(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-hypertension-vignettes"]={"title":"Hypertension Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 60-year-old hypertensive woman has a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio that has risen over three consecutive visits while her estimated filtration rate has fallen. What do these represent?","choices":["Evidence that her blood pressure is adequately controlled","A finding specific to diabetic rather than hypertensive disease","The earliest measurable markers of hypertensive kidney damage","An expected consequence of ageing requiring no action"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and both are watched as trends rather than single values, because the direction of travel is what identifies progression early enough to act on. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man on an angiotensin receptor blocker and a dihydropyridine remains above goal at maximally tolerated doses. What is the next step?","choices":["Add a thiazide-type diuretic","Add an ACE inhibitor","Add spironolactone now","Add a second dihydropyridine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The foundational three are a renin-angiotensin blocker, a dihydropyridine and a thiazide-type diuretic, all titrated before resistance can be considered confirmed. It is the third of the foundational agents. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 60-year-old hypertensive man has an electrocardiogram that does not meet voltage criteria for left ventricular hypertrophy, but he is breathless on exertion and has a significant murmur. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Proceed to echocardiography","Conclude that hypertensive heart disease is excluded","Repeat the electrocardiogram in six months","Order a chest radiograph to measure the cardiac size"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The voltage criteria are specific but NOT sensitive, so false negatives are common. Unexplained dyspnoea and a significant murmur are both indications for echocardiography in their own right. A normal tracing does not exclude hypertrophy. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man remains at 158/96 on three agents at maximally tolerated doses. What is the commonest explanation to exclude first?","choices":["Undiagnosed primary aldosteronism","An incorrectly sized cuff","Progressive renal artery stenosis","Medication nonadherence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it heads a list that also includes measurement error, white coat effect, high sodium or alcohol intake, interfering drugs, and undiagnosed sleep apnoea or secondary hypertension. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 44-year-old newly diagnosed hypertensive patient asks why treatment matters if she feels well. What is the strongest answer?","choices":["Feeling well means her risk is already low","It causes cumulative organ damage with no reliable symptoms","She will begin to feel symptoms within a few months if untreated","Treatment is optional until organ damage is demonstrated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The heart, brain, kidneys, aorta, arteries and retina are all injured over years, and most patients have no reliable blood pressure-related symptoms to warn them. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Why is fundoscopy performed in every newly diagnosed hypertensive patient?","choices":["It allows direct visualisation of the microvasculature","It distinguishes primary from secondary hypertension","It replaces the need for renal and cardiac assessment","It determines which antihypertensive class to start"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the findings carry prognostic weight beyond the reading itself: retinopathy predicts long-term stroke risk, retinal arterial narrowing predicts future hypertension, and control can produce regression. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman on two agents has an average blood pressure of 134/84. What should happen?","choices":["Intensify therapy, since the goal is below 130/80","Accept this as at goal, since it is below 140/90","Stop one agent, since she is close to goal","Accept whatever value she tolerates without symptoms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and values closer to 120/80 are encouraged where appropriate. The same threshold defines the condition and measures control. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 54-year-old arrives late for a routine visit, is taken straight to the room and has her blood pressure measured immediately while she describes her morning. The cuff is placed over her sleeve and her legs are crossed. The reading is 152/94. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat it after five minutes of quiet rest","Diagnose stage 2 hypertension and start two agents","Order plasma metanephrines to exclude pheochromocytoma","Reassure her that a single elevated reading is meaningless"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Every departure here inflates the reading: no rest period, talking during the measurement, a sleeved arm and crossed legs. A diagnosis rests on readings taken to standard, and acting on this one would start treatment on an artefact. Correct technique matters as much as the rest period. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man arrives with blood pressure 232/128, confusion and blurred vision. Over the first hour, how far should his mean arterial pressure be reduced?","choices":["By no more than about twenty to twenty-five per cent","To below 130/80 within the first hour","By at least half, to remove the immediate danger","No reduction until imaging is complete"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, targeting around 160/100 to 110 over two to six hours and gradual normalisation over a day or two. Autoregulation has adapted to the higher pressure, so dropping it to a chronic goal acutely can cause ischaemic stroke, myocardial injury or renal failure. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman presents with acute right hemiparesis and aphasia of two hours' duration. Her blood pressure is 196/104. How should her blood pressure be managed?","choices":["Leave the blood pressure entirely untreated","Follow the acute stroke protocol, not chronic targets","Reduce the pressure to below 130/80 immediately","Reduce the mean arterial pressure by a quarter in an hour"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute stroke is specifically excepted from the general emergency reduction approach, because driving the pressure to a chronic target can extend the ischaemic injury. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man on chlorthalidone presents with an acutely painful, swollen first metatarsophalangeal joint. What connects this to his medication?","choices":["Thiazide-induced angioedema","Thiazide-induced ankle oedema","Hyperuricaemia may precipitate gout","Thiazide-induced hyperkalaemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why uric acid is monitored alongside electrolytes and volume status in patients on a thiazide-type diuretic. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with long-standing hypertension has echocardiography showing left ventricular hypertrophy with impaired diastolic relaxation and a normal ejection fraction. He is breathless on exertion. What does this mean for his prognosis?","choices":["A normal ejection fraction excludes heart failure","The hypertrophy carries no risk of its own","Valvular stenosis is the likely explanation","Preserved ejection fraction failure is a recognised endpoint"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A normal ejection fraction does not reassure, because preserved-fraction failure is one of the two routes hypertensive heart disease can take. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 58-year-old hypertensive man has abrupt severe tearing chest pain radiating to his back, maximal at onset. His right arm pressure is 176/92 and his left is 138/78. A chest radiograph is reported as normal. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Obtain immediate cross-sectional vascular imaging","Reassure him, since the chest radiograph is normal","Obtain an echocardiogram to establish the diagnosis","Delay imaging until the blood pressure is controlled"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A normal chest radiograph does NOT exclude dissection, so a normal film must not end the workup. The pain that is maximal at onset and the asymmetric arm pressures both point to it, and surgical involvement is urgent. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 49-year-old newly diagnosed hypertensive patient has fundoscopy showing generalised arteriolar narrowing and arteriovenous nicking, with no haemorrhages or exudates. What does this tell you?","choices":["These findings indicate a hypertensive emergency","These findings carry no prognostic weight","They reflect current and previous pressure","These changes reflect current blood pressure only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Generalised narrowing and nicking record the accumulated burden, whereas focal narrowing, haemorrhages and exudates relate to CURRENT pressure only. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has an average home blood pressure of 126/78. He has no diabetes, no kidney disease and no cardiovascular history. What should he be advised?","choices":["Lifestyle treatment","No intervention is needed until he reaches stage 1","Start a thiazide-type diuretic now","Recheck in five years with no advice in the interim"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An elevated reading that does not yet warrant medication still warrants an intervention, and lifestyle measures continue alongside drugs later rather than being replaced by them. It is recommended at every blood pressure category. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man's blood pressure is 148/88 in the right arm and 132/80 in the left at his first visit. He has no chest pain and his pulses are symmetric and normal. Which arm should be used at follow-up visits?","choices":["Compare arm and leg pressures to exclude coarctation","Use the right arm for all subsequent measurements","Average the two readings and record 140/84","Image immediately for aortic dissection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both arms are measured at the initial visit precisely to find a difference, and the higher consistent arm becomes the one used for follow-up. Averaging the two would systematically understate his pressure. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man needs two agents. Which pairing is appropriate?","choices":["A beta blocker with verapamil","Two thiazide-type diuretics together","An angiotensin receptor blocker with a dihydropyridine","An ACE inhibitor with an angiotensin receptor blocker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The preferred combinations pair a renin-angiotensin blocker with either a dihydropyridine or a thiazide-type diuretic, because the classes act by complementary mechanisms. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with hypertension and atrial fibrillation is on metoprolol. A colleague suggests adding verapamil for rate control and blood pressure. What is the concern?","choices":["Rebound hypertension on withdrawal","Severe bradycardia and heart block","Hyperkalaemia and acute kidney injury","Dependent ankle oedema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both agents slow the sinus and atrioventricular nodes, so combining them stacks the same effect. Both are also avoided where the ejection fraction is already reduced. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman is referred with hypertension diagnosed at 32 and an adrenal nodule found incidentally on imaging. What is the underlying abnormality if aldosteronism is confirmed?","choices":["Catecholamine excess from an adrenal medullary tumour","Autonomous aldosterone production independent of renin","Excess renin driving aldosterone production","Glucocorticoid excess promoting sodium retention"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The adrenal gland secretes aldosterone without the usual renin-angiotensin signal, so sodium is retained and potassium wasted regardless of volume status. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man says he can tell when his blood pressure is high because he gets a headache. How should this be addressed?","choices":["Headache is a reliable indicator and can guide his dosing","Absence of headache confirms his pressure is controlled","Any headache requires emergency evaluation","Most patients have no reliable blood pressure-related symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Headache is common but nonspecific and cannot be relied on to indicate the pressure, which is why the diagnosis and its control rest on measurement rather than on how he feels. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man on three separate antihypertensive tablets admits he often forgets the midday dose. What change is most likely to help?","choices":["Adding a fourth agent to compensate","Switching to a beta blocker","Accepting his current control as the best achievable","A single-pill combination"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Where two agents are started, a single-pill combination is preferred precisely because adherence is itself one of the main determinants of whether control is achieved. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which hypertensive patient warrants echocardiography?","choices":["Every newly diagnosed hypertensive patient","Only patients whose medication has already failed","Only patients who cannot tolerate a chest radiograph","One with suspected heart failure and a significant murmur"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside hypertrophy on the electrocardiogram or unexplained dyspnoea. It is not required for every new hypertensive patient. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which pattern of hypertension should prompt a search for a secondary cause?","choices":["Blood pressure that responds well to a single first-line agent","Elevated readings confined to the clinic setting","Early or abrupt onset, resistance, or lost control","Gradual elevation over many years in an older adult"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Each departs from the gradual course expected of primary hypertension, which is what makes the pattern itself the clue rather than any single reading. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman starting home monitoring calls alarmed because one reading was 168/102, although her seven-day average is 128/80. What should she be told?","choices":["Tell her home readings are unreliable and to stop monitoring","Send her to the emergency department","Reassure her that the average matters, not one reading","Increase her medication on the basis of the highest reading"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Overreacting to an outlier generates anxiety and unnecessary changes in therapy, which is why the schedule is built around averaging fourteen readings. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman is started on chlorthalidone. Which monitoring matters most for her?","choices":["No monitoring, since chlorthalidone is well tolerated","Sodium, given the risk of thiazide hyponatraemia","Liver enzymes every three months","Thyroid function every six months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, particularly where sodium is already low-normal, and volume status matters in older adults too. Electrolytes and uric acid are the standing monitoring for the class. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 68-year-old smoker with peripheral arterial disease has blood pressure of 168/94 despite three agents. An abdominal bruit is audible, ultrasound shows a right kidney 2 cm smaller than the left, and he has had two episodes of sudden pulmonary oedema. What is the most likely underlying cause?","choices":["Chronic kidney disease from diabetes","Obstructive sleep apnoea","Renovascular disease","Primary aldosteronism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The cluster is characteristic: resistant pressure, an abdominal bruit, asymmetric kidneys and recurrent flash pulmonary oedema. In an older patient with established vascular disease the mechanism is atherosclerotic stenosis, often bilateral and progressive. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 64-year-old hypertensive man also has a raised low-density lipoprotein. What principle should guide his management?","choices":["Lipid therapy is unnecessary once blood pressure reaches goal","Treat total cardiovascular risk, not pressure alone","Treat the blood pressure and address lipids only if it fails","Treat the lipids first and defer blood pressure control"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypertension accelerates atherosclerosis through shear stress, intimal injury, lipid oxidation and smooth muscle proliferation, so antiplatelet and lipid-lowering therapy are considered where indicated. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with resistant hypertension is started on lisinopril. Her creatinine rises markedly within a week. What does this suggest?","choices":["Expected drug effect requiring no change in approach","Progression of underlying diabetic nephropathy","Acute interstitial nephritis from the medication","Bilateral renal artery stenosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Where perfusion depends on angiotensin-mediated efferent arteriolar tone, blocking it drops the filtration pressure sharply. This is one of the recognised clues to renovascular disease, and it is why the agent is contraindicated in bilateral stenosis. A haemodynamically significant unilateral stenosis does the same. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with stage 1 hypertension asks what dietary and activity changes will help. What should she be told?","choices":["Sodium restriction alone, with no activity component","A high-protein diet with unrestricted sodium","Moderate alcohol intake for cardiovascular benefit","A DASH-style pattern with reduced sodium and exercise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pattern is rich in fruit, vegetables, low-fat dairy and whole grains, the activity target is about 150 minutes a week of moderate aerobic work with resistance training, and a sustained weight loss of five to ten kilograms can meaningfully lower systolic pressure. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with a hypertensive emergency has her pressure reduced from 240/130 to 125/75 within thirty minutes. She then develops a new hemiparesis. What happened?","choices":["Hyperkalaemia precipitated the event","Autoregulation had adapted to the higher pressure","The reduction was appropriate and the deficit is unrelated","She developed rebound hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her organs had reset the range over which they protect their own perfusion, so a value that would be safe in anyone else left her brain underperfused. Reducing it that fast left her brain underperfused. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 69"}]};
